<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Fitzhamon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1107</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Fitzhamon was a Norman baron who conquered the Welsh kingdom of Morgannwg around 1093, overthrowing its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iestyn-ap-gwrgant">Iestyn ap Gwrgant</a>. He built a castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a> inside the old Roman fort and granted the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-glamorgan">Vale of Glamorgan</a> to his knights, founding the Lordship of Glamorgan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-fitzhamon.jpg" alt="Robert Fitzhamon" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RobertFitzHamon_BodleianMS_TopGloucD2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Fitzhamon, Seigneur de Creully and Torigny in Normandy, was probably born in the 1040s or 1050s, the son of Hamo Dapifer, Sheriff of Kent, and grandson of Hamon Dentatus. His grandfather held Torigny, Creully, Mézy and Évrecy, but after his death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-val-es-dunes">Battle of Val-ès-Dunes</a> in 1047 the family may have lost these lordships. Robert is said to have been a kinsman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, though how is unknown. He probably did not fight at the Battle of Hastings and does not appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Robert first appears as a supporter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> in the Rebellion of 1088. As a reward he received the feudal barony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, more than two hundred manors that had belonged to Queen Matilda. He refounded Tewkesbury Abbey, apparently under the influence of his wife Sybil de Montgomery, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-de-montgomery">Roger of Montgomery</a>, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>. A 16th-century legend, the Twelve Knights of Glamorgan, tells that Iestyn called in his help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr">Rhys ap Tewdwr</a> and was then dispossessed. Whatever its truth, Robert took the lowlands of Glamorgan and Gwynllwg, holding Cardiff Castle on the site of a Roman fort and new castles at Newport and Kenfig.</p><p>He stayed loyal to Henry I in his conflicts with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a> and was one of three barons who negotiated their truce of 1101. Legend says he wept over the body of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> and covered it with his cloak.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1105 Robert was captured fighting near his family's estates by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a>, one reason Henry I crossed to Normandy with a large force that year. Freed, he joined the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falaise">Falaise</a>, where he suffered a severe head wound; he lived two more years but was never the same in mind. He was buried in the chapter house at Tewkesbury. His eldest daughter Mabel inherited his estates and married Robert de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>, a natural son of Henry I, who was made 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> in 1122.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Fitzhamon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The region later fell to the Normans under <b>Robert Fitzhamon</b> in the 1090s.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-glywysing">Kingdom of Glywysing</a></li><li><blockquote>Iestyn ap Gwrgant, the last native ruler, was overthrown around 1090 by the Norman <b>Robert Fitzhamon</b>, who created the Lordship of Glamorgan.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-morgannwg">Kingdom of Morgannwg</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1093 <b>Robert Fitzhamon</b> overthrew Iestyn ap Gwrgant of the Kingdom of Morgannwg and built a castle at Cardiff inside the old Roman fort.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-glamorgan">Lordship of Glamorgan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Creully, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Falaise, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.28,-0.54&amp;year=1097" aria-label="Creully on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Robert Fitzhamon"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4970 5911h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4970 5911h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.28,-0.54&amp;year=1097">What was here in 1097? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
